.TH DCTEST 1
.SH NAME
dctest \- regression test for dc(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dctest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dctest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/dc.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(argv,\ stdintext) ,
and checks it against a real, live oracle: plan9port's own
.B dc
binary, run through this emu's host-command-execution device
.RB ( #C ,
see
.IR cmd (3)),
given the SAME stdin text (and, for one case, the same file argument)
on both sides.
.PP
Unlike every other
.B p9/cmd
translation's own test suite so far, dc scripts are normally driven
entirely over standard input rather than a host filename, so the
oracle helper here pipes a string directly into the real
.BR dc 's
stdin via
.BR /cmd/ N /data
opened for writing \(em closed immediately afterward (dropping the
last Limbo reference, which synchronously closes the underlying pipe,
since Dis's memory model is reference-counted rather than a deferred
tracing garbage collector) so the real
.B dc
sees genuine end-of-file on its own stdin and can finish, rather than
blocking forever waiting for more input that will never arrive. This
was found the hard way, by a genuine hang during this suite's own
development, not anticipated from
.IR cmd (3)'s
own documentation \(em see
.BR dc.b 's
header comment and the
.B inferno-rio-cmd-device-oracle
memory for the full account.
.PP
One case
.RB ( t10filethenstdin )
also exercises the confirmed "file argument, then fall back to
stdin" behavior, writing a real host file under
.B /dctest
(via
.IR hostroot() ,
copied from
.IR prtest (1)/ hoctest (1)'s
own convention) and passing it as
.BR run() 's
own
.I argv
on the Inferno side, a matching
.B dir
directive on the oracle side.
.PP
Every case compares stdout byte-exact \(em dc's own arithmetic is
exact fixed-point decimal on both sides, so unlike
.IR hoc (1)'s
own floating-point
.B %.17g
divergence, no numeric-tolerance comparison is needed anywhere here.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/dctest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dc (1)
.SH BUGS
.B t11crashsafety
deliberately does NOT compare against the oracle: the real plan9port
.B dc
SIGSEGVs (confirmed directly, exit 139) on
.BR "5 :r" \(em
too few values on the stack for
.BR : \(em
since the original writes the new array register's table entry before
checking whether the value to store was actually available. That case
only checks that this port's own seam completes without crashing (an
emu process crash would abort this whole suite, not just fail one
check), not that its output matches a process that cannot finish.
.PP
.B t9shellout
similarly only checks SHAPE (that the host command actually ran and
that the trailing
.B !
banner appeared), never comparing byte-exact against the oracle: the
real
.B dc
forks
.BR /bin/rc ,
this port runs the host's own
.B sh
instead (see
.IR dc (1)'s
own BUGS), so the two sides' output can never match by construction.
.PP
Does not exercise
.BR ? 's
own interactive-read-bypassing-macros behavior (needs a real terminal-
like stdin interleaving this seam's single up-front
.I stdintext
string can't easily represent), nested
.B x
recursion deeper than the one factorial case, or the
.B Y
debug command's own (deliberately non-original-format, see
.BR dc.b 's
own header comment) allocator-counter output.
